  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following hydrogen bonds is likely to be the weakest?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "pwnT0",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H} \\cdots \\mathrm{O}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "SzTuj",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H} \\cdots \\mathrm{O}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AhjSV",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H} \\cdots \\mathrm{O}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5nBCF",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H} \\cdots \\mathrm{F}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "For $x-\\stackrel{\\delta}{H}^{+} \\cdots \\stackrel{\\delta-}{O}$ hydrogen bond; lower the electronegativity of $x$; lower is the residual $\\delta+$ change induced on $\\mathrm{H}$ & hence a lesser electrostatic attraction is expected between $\\mathrm{H}$ & $\\mathrm{O}$; thus diminishing bond strength. Hence, chosen option is the atom of lowest electronegativity, i.e. C.",
    "image": null
  },
